# Files Nx must not treat as workspace input.
#
# .gitignore — AND THE REASON IS NOT WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE.
#
# Nx's file walker honours gitignore rules, and that includes the developer's
# GLOBAL one (core.excludesFile). A machine whose global ignore file lists
# `.gitignore` itself never lets Nx see the repo's `.gitignore` — while a Linux
# CI runner, which usually has no global ignore file, sees it and folds it into
# the hash of EVERY task.
#
# Measured on the upstream project this came from (16 Aug 2026) by dumping the
# hash plan for all 70 lint+typecheck tasks on both machines: the plans were
# identical except for `file:.gitignore`, present on Linux and absent on macOS,
# on every single task. One phantom input is enough to change every task hash,
# which makes a cache entry computed on a laptop unusable on the runner — and
# that exchange is the whole point of ops/ci/seed-cache.ts.
#
# Declaring it in nx.json's sharedGlobals does NOT fix this: a machine whose
# walker cannot see the file resolves the entry to nothing, so the laptop stays
# where it is and the runner counts it twice. Excluding it works from both ends,
# because "do not look at this" is something both machines can obey.
#
# What it costs: editing `.gitignore` no longer invalidates cached tasks. That is
# already how a laptop with a global ignore behaves, so this makes CI agree with
# it. Run `nx reset` after a `.gitignore` change that is meant to change what Nx
# can see.
.gitignore
